## 1. Introduction
As a serious malignancy, cervical cancer remains a great threat to female, and the incidence rate is increasing among younger people [1,2]. The combination of surgery, radiotherapy, and chemotherapy are effective methods to treat cervical cancer [3]. However, the side toxicity, recrudesce, and overall survival time of patients remain a challenge [4]. Starvation therapy mediated by GOx has shown great achievements, which is considered as an emerging green therapy method without using any chemotherapeutics [5]. Starvation therapy greatly interferes with glucose metabolism, and the fast consumption of glucose effectively blocks energy supply and then induces cell death [6]. A recent study has found that GOx can cut off energy supply inside cancer cells but cannot block nutrient supply from the body [7]. Therefore, starvation therapy can only inhibit the growth of cancer cells rather than eliminate them completely. On the other hand, GOx is difficult to be endocytosed by cells, and thus incubating cancer cells with pure GOx shows poor therapeutic effect [8]. Therefore, the delivery of sufficient amount of GOx into cells and efficient combination of other therapy methods are the two main factors determining the final therapeutic effect.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands with high porosity, tunable pore size, and huge specific surface area [9,10]. MOFs have promising applications in gas storage, catalysis, biosensing, and drug delivery [11–15]. However, MOFs are easily aggregated in water, which results in their large dispersed size and extra toxic side effects [16,17]. In addition, the instability of MOFs in water often leads to skeleton collapse during drug delivery [18]. At present, the dispersibility and stability of MOFs in water are primarily improved through surface modification. Han et al. reported a hybrid MOF structure by improving its water dispersity through dopamine polymerization and modification [19]. Xia et al. introduced hydrophilic polymer with moderate molecular weight to enhance the surface wettability and water dispersibility of porphyrin-based $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF (PCN-222) [20]. Zhu et al. provided a strategy to introduce alkyl chains into organic linkers, which not only protected hydrophilic $Z r_{6}O_{8}$ clusters of UiO-67, but also displayed high acid, base, and water stability [21]. Therefore, the water dispersibility of MOFs can be improved by hydrophilic modification, whereas water stability can be enhanced by the introduction of hydrophobic groups. However, surface modification of various MOFs using GOx to improve water monodispersibility and stability while maintaining anticancer activity remains to be a great challenge.
Herein, a nanoplatform of GOx and Ag nanoparticles $\left({\sf A g N P s}\right)$ functionalized MOFs with great water dispersibility and stability was developed for cancer starvation and photothermal therapy (Fig. 1). Our strategy could be effectively applied to functionalize different types of MOFs, such as UiO-66, MOF808, HKUST-1, MOF-5, MIL-125, and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125. GOx chain was coordinated on unsaturated metal sites of MOFs surface by using phenolic hydroxyl groups of tyrosine residues to form a homogeneous GOx layer with high efficiency. Moreover, large amounts of hydrophilic groups in GOx ensure excellent water dispersibility and hydrophilicity, allowing the uniform deposition of Ag NPs on the surface $(\mathsf{M O F s@G O x@A g})$ . In vitro and in vivo experiments illustrate that the synergistic therapy has a good therapeutic effect at low doses. Synergistic treatment also effectively inhibits cancer cell migration and induces cell apoptosis by generating reactive oxygen species (ROS). Thus, the non-invasive synergistic treatment model of our $\mathrm{MOFs@\cdot}$ $\mathsf{G O x@A g}$ nanosystem has built a more efficient cancer treatment platform.
## 2. Experimental details
## 2.1. Raw materials
All chemical reagents were used without further purification. Zirconyl chloride octahydrate (AR), 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (AR) were purchased from Energy Chemical . Acetic acid (AR) and Polyvinylpyrrolidone (GR) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. N, N’-dimethylformamide (DMF) was purchased from Shannxi Dexiang Chemical. Glucose oxidase (GOx) was purchased from Shanghai Yuanye Bio-Technology Co., Ltd. Silver nitrate (AR) was purchased from Shanghai Senhao Fina Chemcals Co., Ltd. Sodium borohydride (AR) was purchased from Shanghai Zhanyun Chemical Co., Ltd. HeLa cells and BEAS-2B cells were purchased from Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences. High glucose DMEM medium was purchased from Beijing Solarblo Technology Co., Ltd. Rhodamine B (RhB) was purchased from Tianjin Beilian Fine Chemicals Development Co., Ltd. Methyl thiazolyl Tetrazolium (MTT) and Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) were purchased from Shanghai Sangon Bioengineering Co., Ltd. Trypsin, Annexin V-FITC/PI Apoptosis Assay Kit, Reactive Oxygen Species Detection Kit were all purchased from Shanghai Beyotime Biotechnology. Deionized water $(>18~\mathrm{M}\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) was obtained from ultrapure water machine.
## 2.2. Preparation of the different types of MOFs
UiO-66: UiO-66 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [22]. Zirconyl chloride octahydrate $(315~\mathrm{~mg},$ $0.99\mathrm{\mmol}$ ) and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid ( $750\mathrm{mg}$ , 4.5 mmol) were dissolved in DMF $45~\mathrm{mL}$ and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ ) respectively. The two solutions were mixed together, and then $9.6~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was added. After sonicating for $2~\mathrm{min}$ , the solution was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ reaction kettle and heated at $90~^{\circ}C$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain UiO-66. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with DMF and methanol (11000 rpm, $10\ \mathrm{min}$ for three times respectively and followed by drying at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to yield UiO-66 powder.
MOF-808: MOF-808 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [23]. Zirconyl chloride octahydrate ( $945~\mathrm{\mg}$ $3.00\quad\mathrm{mmol};$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $(207\quad{\mathrm{mg}},$ $1.00\ \mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ) were dissolved in DMF $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ). $50~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was added into the mixture. After sonicating for $2\mathrm{min}$ , the solution was transferred to a reaction kettle and heated at $130^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to obtain MOF-808. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with DMF and acetone $(4500\ \mathrm{rpm},15\ \mathrm{min})$ for three times respectively and followed by drying in a desiccator for two days to yield MOF-808 powder.
HKUST-1: HKUST-1 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [24]. Copper nitrate trihydrate (484 mg, $2.00\quad\mathrm{mmol}_{.}^{\cdot}$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $(232\mod$ $1.10\ \mathrm{mmol}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ) were dissolved in EtOH $\mathrm{:}12~\mathrm{mL}$ and ultrapure water $\cdot12\mathrm{mL},$ respectively. The two solutions were mixed together. After sonicating for $2\:\mathrm{min}$ , the solution was transferred to a reaction kettle and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain HKUST-1. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with ultrapure water and EtOH (6000 rpm, $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) for three times respectively and followed by drying at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to yield HKUST-1 powder.
MOF-5: MOF-5 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [25]. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate (290 mg, $1.00\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\left(80\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.50\ \mathrm{mmol}\right),$ were dissolved in DMF $\mathrm{20~mL}$ . The solution was transferred to a reaction kettle and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ to obtain MOF-5. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with DMF and chloroform (8000 rpm, $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) for three times respectively and followed by drying at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to yield MOF-5 powder.
MIL-125: MIL-125 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [26]. Tetrabutyl titanate $(1.2~\mathrm{\mL})$ and 1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid $(1.9\mathrm{~g},6.1\mathrm{~mmol})$ were dissolved in a mixture containing DMF $18~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and MeOH ( $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ). After sonicating for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ , the solution was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction kettle and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain MIL-125. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with methanol (8000 rpm, $10~\mathrm{{min}}^{\cdot}$ ) for three times and followed by drying at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to yield MIL-125 powder.
$\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125: $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 was synthesized and purified according to previous reports [26]. Tetrabutyl titanate $(1.2~\mathrm{mL})$ 1 and 2-aminoterephtalic acid $(1.1~\mathrm{g},6.1~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in a mixture containing DMF ( $18~\mathrm{mL}$ and MeOH (2 mL). After sonicating for 5 min, the solution was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction kettle and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $48~\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125. Purification was carried out by centrifugation with methanol (8000 rpm, $10\ \mathrm{min}$ ) for three times and followed by drying at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to yield $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 powder.
## 2.3. Preparation of MOFs@GOx
$4\:\mathrm{mg}$ of GOx was dissolved in $5\mathrm{ml}$ of deionized water and then 2 mg MOFs was added into the solution and sonicated at $300\mathsf{W}$ for 20 min. The mixed solution was stirred on a magnetic stirrer at 20 rad $s^{-1}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. Then the solution was centrifuged to remove free GOx from the upper liquid layer at 11000 rpm for 10 min. The collected solid products were washed with deionized water two times. Eventually, MOFs@GOx powder was obtained by vacuum freeze-drying.
## 2.4. Synthesis of Ag NPs
Chemical reduction method was used to prepare Ag NPs and the prepared Ag NPs was stabilized by polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) to prevent agglomeration [27,28]. $44.9~\mathrm{{mg}}$ PVP was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and sonicated at 300 W for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ . $5.1~\mathrm{mg}$ silver nitrate was dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and then mixed together. $3.0~\mathrm{mg}$ sodium borohydride was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and then added dropwise to the mixed solution under sonication at $300\mathsf{W}$ to obtain Ag NPs solution.
## 2.5. Preparation of UiO-66@GOx@Ag
The Ag NPs were separated by centrifugation $12000~\mathrm{rpm}$ $20\ \mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$ , and washed three times with ethanol and once with deionized water to remove PVP. UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ was obtained by evenly mixed the solution of UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}$ and the solution of Ag NPs according to the ratio of 1:1. The mixed solution was stirred on a magnetic stirrer at 15 rad $s^{-1}$ for $40~\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.6. Characterizations
The morphology and selected area electron diffraction data of the samples were obtained from transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100PLUS). The X-ray diffraction data (XRD) was measured by Rigaku D/MAX-2400. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping were measured by scanning electron microscopy (ZEISS SIGMA HD). Element analysis of the samples was harvested by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (Thermo Scientific Escalab 250Xi). Fourier transformation infrared spectrometer (FTIR) data was obtained from Thermo Nicolet iS5. UV–Vis data was measured by a UV–Visible spectrometer (PERSEE TU-1901). Zeta potential, hydrodynamic size and PDI were tested from dynamic light scattering (DLS, Malvern Zetasizer Nano series). Fluorescence intensity of GOx was tested from Fluorescence spectrophotometer (HITACHI F-4600). Thermogravimetric (TG) curves were measured by DSC/DTA-TG (STA409PC).
## 2.7. Methods
Aqueous dispersibility evaluation: Hydrodynamic size distribution of the MOFs (UiO-66, MOF-808, MOF-5, HKUST-1, MIL125 and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125) and corresponding MOFs@GOx suspended in aqueous media were tested from DLS. Samples suspended in aqueous media were diluted by deionized water and put into a glass cell to take photographs.
Photothermal performance investigation: To investigate the photothermal conversion ability of UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ , a NIR laser (Haoxuer, LDP-808–3000) with a wavelength of $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was used for testing. The laser power was fixed to the power of $2.4\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the concentration of UiO- $-66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ was changed from $0.4~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ to $1.2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ . The temperature was measured by a thermocouple temperature sensor connected to the inside of the glass tube. In order to evaluate the photothermal stability, $1.0~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ of UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ was irradiated on and off at a power density of $2.4~\mathrm{W~cm}^{-2}$ , and the temperature from heating up to cooling was recorded. In addition, thermal image of UiO$66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ $1.2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ ) with $2.4\mathrm{~W~cm}^{-2}$ NIR irradiation for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ was captured by infrared camera.
Cell uptake and intracellular drug release study: Cell uptake of UiO-66 was studied by monitoring the fluorescence intensity of Rhodamine B (RhB) in cells. The doses of RhB in medium were 0.005, 0.01, 0.02 and $0.04\ \mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. After another $24\mathrm{~h~}$ incubation, the cells were washed three times with phosphate buffered saline (PBS) and digested with trypsin, $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ PBS was added to each well, and the wells were centrifuged twice at 3500 rpm for 5 min each time. For flow cytometry (ACEA, Novocyte2040R) tests, the detection channel was FITC-A. To ensure the accuracy of the results, three parallel determinations were performed.
Confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM, Carl Zeiss AG, Germany) was used to visualize fluorescence imaging in vitro for investigating drug release behavior. HeLa cells were preinoculated onto six-well plates, and $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of Dulbecco’s modified eagle medium (DMEM) was added culture medium to each well. Then the cells were incubated at a constant temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $5\%\thinspace{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ humid atmosphere for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Similarly, the mixture of UiO-66 $(50~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})+\mathrm{RhB}~(0.1~\mathrm{mM})$ was stirred for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently the culture medium replaced with fresh ones, and added with pre-prepared UiO- ${\cdot}66+$ RhB, incubated for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After washing the cells on the glass panel with PBS buffer solution $\left(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\right)$ for 5 times, the cells were fixed with PBS buffered paraformaldehyde for $15\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. Washed again with PBS buffered solution 3 times, and cell images were observed under CLSM in the condition that each hole had $3~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS buffered solution.
In vitro cytotoxicity study: The intracellular cytotoxicity of the samples was assessed using the MTT assay technique. Six different groups were set up depending on the incubation environment. To accurately calculate the cell survival rate, the wells without sample addition were used as the control group A. And the cell viability in these groups was defined as $100\%$ during calculation. UiO-66 solution (in-well concentrations of 1, 5, 10, 20, 50 and $100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively) was added in experimental groups B and C. BEAS2B cells and HeLa cells were then placed in an incubator for further incubation, respectively. Experimental group D were supplemented with GOx solution (in-well concentrations of 0.01, 0.02, 0.03 and $0.04~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively). Then experimental groups E and F were supplemented with UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ solution (inwell concentrations of 0.01, 0.02, 0.03 and $0.04~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively). HeLa cells were then placed in an incubator for further incubation. After $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , 96-well plates were taken out and treated with near-infrared light for group F. The parameters were set at $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $2.4\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . The cells continued to incubate at a constant temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Subsequently, $15\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of
$5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ MTT solution was added to each well to form bluepurple crystalline formazan, while $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of dimethyl sulfoxide was added to promote the dissolution of blue-purple formazan, and the plate was gently shaken to dissolve evenly. And a microplate reader (DNM-9606, Perlong Medical, Beijing, China) at an absorbance of $570\ \mathrm{nm}$ was used for detection. The cell viability was calculated by the following formula: Cell viabil= ODOEDxperimentODODBlac . The experiment was carried out three times in parallel to reduce the error, and the statistical average was taken as the final result.
Anti-migration assay: Four paralleled HeLa cells that had close growth state were prepared, then a pipette tip was used to cut monolayer cells along a straight line to form a scratch, recorded the initial scratch width after marking. The four cells were treated as follows, the first was without any treatment, the second was treated with $0.01~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ pure GOx, the third was treated with $0.01~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Ui $){-}66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ , and the fourth was treated with $0.01~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ UiO $-66@\mathrm{GO}\times@\mathrm{Ag}+\mathrm{NIR}$ . After being placed the culture dish in a tissue culture incubator at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the culture dish was placed under the inverted fluorescent digital microscope (Olympus, PENIX73-DP80), the reference point was matched and the scratch width was recorded again. Finally, the specific value of the width was measured by special software.
Apoptosis mechanism assay: Flow cytometry analysis of apoptotic populations of the cells caused by pure GOx, UiO$66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ and UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx@Ag~+~NIR}$ were carried out using the Annexin V-FITC Apoptosis Detection Kit (Beyotime Institute of Biotechnology, China). Briefly, HeLa cells were preincubated in drug-free media at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , after which different samples was added against HeLa cells. After $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ of drug exposure, cells were collected, washed once with PBS, and re-suspended in $195~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of annexin V-FITC binding buffer which was then added to $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ annexin V-FITC and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PI, and incubated at room temperature in the dark for $15{\mathrm{~min}}$ . Subsequently, the buffer placed in an ice bath in the dark. The samples were analyzed by a flow cytometer.
ROS determination: A two-photon laser confocal microscope was used to observe the effect of drugs on ROS production in HeLa cells. HeLa cells were inoculated in 6-well plates and incubated with different samples for $24{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . DCFH-DA was used as a probe to add cells and incubated for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , then washed with serum-free medium for 3 times and added with serum-free medium for observation. The experiment was divided into control group and experimental groups. Add $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ sample to each well of the experimental group and treated for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ The cells were digested with trypsin, centrifuged for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , and the supernatant was taken. Then it was rinsed twice with 0.01 M PBS buffer. The cells deposited at the bottom of the centrifuge tube were again suspended with DCFH-DA dilution. After incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ , the cells were washed with PBS buffer solution and apoptosis was detected by flow cytometry.
In vivo therapeutic effect assay: The healthy female nude mice (body mass $25\ \mathrm{g}$ were provided by Yantai Raphael Biotechnology Co., Ltd. Shandong, China. All animal operations were conducted in accordance with the guidelines of the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of Qufu Normal University (Approval number: no. QFNU2017-917R). The mice were injected subcutaneously with cancer cells. When the tumor size reached approximately $100~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ , the tumor mice were divided into four groups (5 mice per group). The first group were injected subcutaneously with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ pure GOx, the second and third groups were injected subcutaneously with $\operatorname{JiO-}66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ and the third group was then irradiated with NIR after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The concentration of GOx and UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ were both $1.2~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The control groups were injected with $200\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PBS $(0.01\mathrm{~M})$ . The samples were injected once a day for 13 days. The body weight and tumor volume of mice were recorded regularly once a day. Tumor volume (V) was determined by the following equation: $\mathsf{V}=0.5\mathrm{Xy}^{2}$ , where x and y are the length and width of the tumor respectively. At day 14th, four mice were sacrificed and tumors were excised. For pathology examination, the above-collected major organs were routinely fixed in $10\%$ formalin, embedded in paraffin, sliced, stained by hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), and observed using a digital microscope. The thermal image of tumor-bearing mice injected with PBS or UiO$66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ under $808\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser (2.4 W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) irradiation for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization
TEM images (Fig. 2 a1 and Fig. 2 a2) show that the shape of UiO66 is octahedral, with a long axis size of approximately $100\mathrm{nm}$ and a minor axis size of approximately $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ . High-resolution TEM image (Fig. 2 a3) shows that the edges of UiO-66 are relatively sharp. Fig. 2 (b1–b3) show TEM and HRTEM images of UiO$66@\mathsf{G O x}$ . The shape of UiO-66@GOx was changed into sphere and the edges of UiO-66@GOx became rounded because of GOx cladding. The GOx layer on MOFs was clearly observed from the edges (arrow in Fig. 2 b3). TEM and SAED data (Figs. 2 c1–c3) showed that Ag NPs were well deposited onto the surface of UiO$66@\mathrm{{GOx}}$ , with a size of approximately $10\mathrm{nm}$ . The SAED image (inset in Fig. $^{2}\mathbf{c}\mathbf{2}$ ) illustrated the high regularity of the Ag crystal structure. The average lattice distance was $0.20\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (2 0 0) crystal plane [29].
XRD data (Fig. 2d) showed that the diffraction pattern of UiO-66 was consistent with the simulated results. The diffraction pattern of UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ broadened because the surface of UiO-66 was covered by amorphous GOx, which somehow overlapped the crystallinity. Notably, the position of the diffraction peaks did not change, indicating that GOx modification had no effect on the crystal structure of UiO-66. Diffraction peaks at $2\uptheta=37.95^{\circ}$ and $43.08^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (111) and (20 0) crystal planes of the Ag crystal [30], indicating the successful loading of Ag NPs. Meanwhile, SEM and EDS mapping analyses of UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ were also investigated (Fig. 2e). The appearance of nitrogen, phosphorus and silver demonstrated the successful modification of GOx and Ag NPs. The uniform distribution of these elements revealed that GOx and Ag NPs were uniformly covered on the surface of UiO-66.
Fig. 3a shows Zr 3d XPS fine spectra that demonstrate the binding mechanism of UiO-66 with GOx. The Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peak was located at $183.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S1d), corresponding to $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ . Nevertheless, after GOx modification, the peak shifted to $182.4~\mathrm{eV}$ which could be fitted by $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ $(183.1~\mathrm{eV})$ and Zr$0_{\mathsf{G O x}}$ $(182.4~\mathrm{eV})$ [31]. Fig. S1 $(\mathsf{a}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{c})$ shows that zirconium (181.1, 331.4, and $349.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ , carbon $(286.0~\mathrm{eV})$ , and oxygen (532.1 eV) were detected for all products, whereas new peaks at $400.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ which correspond to the binding energy of nitrogen appeared after GOx modification [32]. Ag $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Ag $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks at 368.1 and $373.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ were only observed from UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ , which could be ascribed to metallic silver $(\mathsf{A g}^{0})$ [33]. The peaks of zirconium almost disappeared after GOx loading, which indicated that UiO66 was encapsulated by GOx. The result illustrated that phenolic groups of tyrosine residues on the GOx peptide chain formed coordination bonds with Zr (IV). FTIR data (Fig. 3b) shows that different from UiO-66, a new peak at $1652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ascribing to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching vibration of the peptide in GOx appeared in UiO$66@\mathrm{GOx}$ , indicating the effective preservation of the secondary structure and bioactivity of GOx [34]. As the loading of Ag NPs did not rely on chemical bonds, the spectrums of UiO$66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ and UiO-66@GOx were nearly the same. The abovementioned results indicated the successful coating of GOx on MOFs.
As shown in UV–Vis spectra (Fig. 3c), characteristic peaks of GOx and UiO-66 were located at 215 and $240\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which also appeared simultaneously in UiO-66@GOx and UiO$66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ . The peak at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ was the characteristic surface plasma resonance peak of ${\sf A g}{\sf N P s}$ [35], which was also detected in UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ , indicating the presence of metallic Ag NPs $(\mathsf{A g}^{0})$ . The zeta potential of UiO-66 in water (Fig. 3d) showed a peak at $-28.4\mathrm{mV}$ because of a large number of oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface. After GOx modification, the value changed to $-19.4\mathrm{mV}$ , and the load of Ag NPs further made surface charge more positive $\ensuremath{'}-16.2\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mV}})$ . A large number of oxygencontaining groups on GOx surface served as adsorption sites for Ag NPs, because oxygen groups and Ag could form strong interactions between each other, thereby allowing effective Ag NPs deposition [36].
The modification process was also validated by fluorescence experiment (Fig. 3e). GOx produced a strong fluorescence emission peak at $358~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the fluorescence intensity of GOx gradually decreased with the increase of the amount of UiO-66. The initial concentration of GOx was $1.5\mathrm{\mg}\mathrm{\mL}^{-1}$ , and the fluorescence almost quenched when the concentration of UiO-66 reached $0.6~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ . Increasing the concentration of UiO-66, considerable amount of GOx generated non-luminescent ground-state complexes with UiO-66, which induced fluorescence quenching. UiO66 possessed a high value of $1463~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the Brunauer–Emm ett–Teller (BET) surface area (Fig. S2), and its weight loss (Fig. S3a) was primarily due to water evaporation when the temperature was below $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Almost no weight loss was observed between $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and the framework collapsed when the temperature was higher than $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [37]. The TGA curve of UiO-66@GOx (Fig. S3b) showed evident weight loss from $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ caused by GOx decomposition. Based on TGA data (Fig. 3f and S3), the loading capacity of Ag NPs was calculated as about $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in UiO-66@GOx@Ag.
## 3.2. Dispersal stability and photothermal property
In investigating the dispersion of UiO-66 with and without GOx modification, the hydrodynamic size was measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS). As shown in Fig. 4 (a–b), the hydrodynamic size of UiO-66 in water was $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the PDI index was 0.55, indicating severe particle agglomeration. By contrast, after GOx modification, the size quickly decreased to approximately $100\mathrm{nm}$ with a low PDI index of 0.15, indicating that GOx functionalization remarkably avoided agglomeration. The particle size was consistent with TEM results (Figs. 2 b1 and b2). The loading of $\mathsf{A g}$ NPs had no effect on dispersion, and it even maintained such a process for 3 days. Visible aggregated particles of UiO-66 were observed after 3 days, whereas UiO- $66@\mathrm{{GOx}}$ and UiO$66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ remained clear and transparent (Fig. 4c). The exciting results confirmed the powerful function of GOx in modulating the surface and improving the dispersity of UiO-66.
In evaluating the universality of the method that modifying different MOFs with stable monodispersity, MOF-808, HKUST-1, MOF-5, MIL-125 and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 were selected and modified using GOx. As shown in Fig. S4, a significant sedimentation or delamination of MOFs was observed. Among them, HKUST-1 has poor stability in water [38]. This MOF decomposed within a few minutes after water addition, resulting in a large amount of flocculated precipitation. However, its dispersibility was significantly improved after GOx modification and remained stable in water without decomposition. Moreover, without GOx modification, UiO-66, MOF-808, HKUST-1, MOF-5, MIL-125 and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 were approximately 400, 1700, 2000, 800, 850, and $1250~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in size, respectively, indicating severe agglomeration and poor dispersibility. After GOx modification, sizes decreased dramatically to 100, 125, 400, 400, 200, and $375\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Monodispersion indicates that a homogenous GOx coating layer is formed, which protects the open metal sites; thus, $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ cannot coordinate with metals. GOx modification does not replace the original coordination bonds and does not induce collapse of the framework, which provides a new idea to resolve the poor water dispersion and stability problems of various MOFs.
Ag NPs have a good photothermal effect resulting from the rapid conversion of free electrons resonant oscillation into heat during illumination [39]. In investigating whether the property was inherited, the photothermal conversion performance was investigated in detail. As shown in Fig. 4d and Fig. 4f, the temperature of Ui ${\scriptstyle0-66@60\mathrm{x}@\mathrm{Ag}}$ solution could easily reach $57^{\circ}C$ when exposed to near-infrared (NIR) light, and such a temperature could greatly enhance the efficacy of drug toxicity toward cancer cells [40]. Cycle heating and cooling curve (Fig. 4e) reflected that UiO$66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ showed good stability upon NIR illumination, which laid a solid foundation for future practical applications.
## 3.3. In vitro anticancer effect and related mechanism
As shown in Fig. S5, as the concentration of UiO- $66+{\tt R h B}$ gradually increased, the fluorescence intensity increased accordingly, indicating that the amount of UiO- $66+{\tt R h B}$ in cells was proportional to the concentration, and the size of UiO-66 was suitable for cell endocytic uptake. The result was also confirmed by CLSM (Fig. 5a). Weak fluorescence intensity was found after treating cells for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ , and the intensity was significantly enhanced after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , indicating that RhB was gradually and intracellularly released with prolonged time.
The low cytotoxicity of nanocarrier is a key factor for further biological applications [41–43]. As shown in Fig. S6 (a–b), the cytotoxicity of UiO-66 was evaluated by MTT assay, and it showed nontoxicity to BEAS-2B and HeLa cells. The cell viability remained close to $100\%$ even at a high concentration of $100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The anticancer effect (Fig. 5b) indicated no significant cell death with the increase of pure GOx concentration compared with the control group. GOx could not be internalized by cells because of their large molecular size and electrostatic repulsion with negatively charged cell membranes [44]. On the contrary, the cell viability of HeLa cells gradually decreased with the increase of UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ concentration (GOx as benchmark). The anticancer phenomenon became more evident after being irradiated with $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ NIR. Cell viability decreased to about $5\%$ when the GOx concentration was at a low value of $0.04~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The photothermal effect induced by high temperature weakened cell viability [45]. Meanwhile, hyperthermia induced by PTT could also accelerate the uptake of UiO-66 and promote GOx release, which ensured effective starvation/photothermal synergistic therapy.
Metastasis often results in the incapability of being treated completely [46]. Therefore, a highly effective anti-migration effect is essential for complete cancer treatment. In vitro scratch assay was used to evaluate the anti-migration effect. HeLa cells in the control group were substantially healed after $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ culture, and such cells showed a high migration tendency (Fig. 5c). By contrast, the migration rate was assigned as $100\%$ . Pure GOx treatment showed a high migration rate of $81.9\%$ , whereas the migration rate of cells treated with UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ decreased dramatically to $52.4\%$ , and the migration rate further decreased to $38.0\%$ when irradiated with NIR light (Fig. S7). Good anti-migration effectively inhibited the spread of HeLa cells and greatly reduced the risk of tumor metastasis, showing important implications for prognosis.
In investigating related anti-cancer mechanism, cell apoptosis test was conducted. Compared with the control group, GOx could not cause massive apoptosis in HeLa cells (Fig. 5d), whereas the apoptotic rate increased to $36.6\%$ in cancer cells treated with
UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ , and late apoptosis accounted for $28.5\%$ . Moreover, after NIR treatment, the apoptotic rate remarkably increased to $64.9\%$ and primarily concentrated in late apoptosis. The result showed that $\mathsf{A g}$ NPs-mediated PTT could not only kill cancer cells by increasing temperature, but also effectively transport GOx into cells to exert starvation therapy, which finally accelerated apoptosis and achieved enhanced anticancer effects at low GOx concentrations.
GOx generates gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by catalyzing the reaction of glucose and oxygen in cells, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposes to OH under $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ NIR irradiation [47,48]. Compared with the control group, fluorescence intensity slightly increased after adding GOx in HeLa cells for $24\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. 5e), indicating that only a small amount of GOx entered cells and limited ROS was generated. After treating by UiO- ${\it66@60x@60}{\mathrm{_{Ag}}}$ ROS content remarkably increased. When irradiated with $808\ \mathrm{nm}$ NIR, the ROS content greatly increased because of the superior photothermal conversion performance of Ag NPs. High concentrations of ROS destroy cell membranes or DNA in HeLa cells and activate apoptosis, thereby achieving good anti-cancer effect [49].
## 3.4. In vivo starvation/PTT synergistic therapeutic effect
Fig. 6a shows a schematic representation of the in vivo experimental design. Under NIR irradiation, thermal images of mice (Fig. 6b) and according temperature curve (Fig. 6c) were demonstrated. Body temperature of mice in UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx@Ag+NIR}$ group rose markedly to nearly $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ within $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , demonstrating UiO$66@\operatorname{GOx}@\mathsf{A g}$ stored in the tumor area and possessed good photothermal conversion ability. While temperature changes in the $\mathrm{PBS}+\mathsf{N I R}$ group were not noticeable. After 14 days, histological sections (Fig. 6d) of spleen, kidney, liver and heart showed that the organ tissues maintained normal morphological structure, indicating that UiO- $66@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{Ag}$ had no obvious toxic side effects. Tumor tissues of Ui $\scriptstyle\phantom{=}0{\mathrm{-}}66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ and UiO- $66@\mathrm{GO}\mathrm{x@Ag~+~NIR}$ groups showed significant gaps, indicating robust therapeutic effect. Continuous monitoring of weight changes (Fig. 6e) found that mice treated with UiO- $66@\mathrm{GO}\times@\mathrm{Ag}+\mathrm{NIR}$ gained more weight than that in other groups, demonstrating that the synergistic treatment was beneficial to tumor treatment and ensured normal growth of mice. Additionally, changes of tumor volume (Fig. 6f) showed that tumors of mice that injected with PBS, pure GOx and UiO- $66@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{Ag}$ grew up within 14 days. Only tumors treated with UiO $-66@\mathrm{GO}\times@\mathrm{Ag}+\mathrm{NIR}$ were heavily inhibited, proving that synergistic treatment could inhibit tumor growth and had much better anti-cancer effects than starvation therapy alone. Thus, in vivo experiments proved that synergistic therapy had promise and advantages in practical applications, showing great potential for development of novel cancer treatment model.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, a versatile strategy of constructing bioenzymemodified MOFs with stable monodispersity was reported, which was integrated as a non-invasive platform for efficient cancer synergistic therapy. By coordinating interactions between phenolic hydroxyl groups on the chain and unsaturated metal sites of MOFs, GOx formed a homogeneous coating layer on the surface and ensured long-term water stability of MOFs. Abundant hydrophilic groups in GOx ensured good mono-dispersity of $\mathsf{M O F s@G O x}$ and allowed the uniform deposition of ${\sf A g N P s}$ on the hybrids for superior NIR conversion performance, which enabled the construction of starvation/photothermal synergistic therapy. Moreover, in vitro and in vivo experiments illustrated that the nanosystem exhibited better cancer treatment and anti-migration effects compared with single starvation therapy. Compared with previous works [20,50,51], this work provided a new insight into the utilization of a bioenzyme to simultaneously enhance water dispersibility and stability of various MOFs without chemical reactions and complex procedures, and integrating such MOFs for efficient cancer synergistic treatment without any use of chemotherapeutic drugs.
